  "content": {
    "id": "p42-q30-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "30",
    "prompt": "Let the zeroes of $$P(x)=x^3-2x^2+4x-8$$ be $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\gamma$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p42-q30-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the value of $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$-4$",
          "worked": "The root-coefficient relationships give $\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma=2$ and $\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma=4$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2&=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)^2-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\beta\\gamma)\\\\\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2&=2^2-2(4)\\\\\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2&=-4\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q30-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, state the number of $x$-intercepts of the graph of $y=P(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1$ $x$-intercept.\n\nThe negative sum of squares rules out three real roots.",
          "worked": "If all three roots were real, their squares would have a nonnegative sum. Part (a) gives a sum of $-4$, so they are not all real. A cubic with real coefficients has at least one real root, and nonreal roots occur in conjugate pairs. Therefore, it has exactly one real root and the graph has one $x$-intercept.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
